  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "$A$ and $A'$ are reflections of each other across line $l$. What is the relationship between $l$ and $AA'$?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "$l$ is parallel to $AA'$.",
    "B": "$l$ is perpendicular to $AA'$ but does not necessarily bisect it.",
    "C": "$l$ perpendicularly bisects $AA'$.",
    "D": "$l$ and $AA'$ bisect each other but are not necessarily perpendicular."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "For two distinct points that are reflections of each other, the axis of reflection perpendicularly bisects the segment joining them.\n\nIf \\(A\\) lies on \\(l\\), then \\(A'=A\\), and the zero-length segment has no unique supporting line, so perpendicularity is undefined. Under the distinct-point convention \\(A\\ne A'\\), \\(l\\) perpendicularly bisects \\(AA'\\), giving C.",
